@clayjohn

> @Riteo What do you think about this PR with respect to how it will operate with Wayland? Does this make your work any easier? I see that it mostly incorporates https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/68944/files, so that should be helpful

Indeed. The EGL driver used here is actually based on the one I adapted from the original `GLManager` for use in Wayland, with some additions for further EGL configuration, so this would just work there!

@bruvzg please note that, mostly due to rebasing, I've had to fix some things. You can see the latest changes here: https://github.com/Riteo/godot/commits/wayland/drivers/egl

Also note that I've never got to try the `DRI_PRIME` detection code, so I have no idea if it works (I've just adapted it from the original GL manager one). It might be a good idea to try it out before merging.

@bruvzg This is amazing work!

Just as a bit of context, there is still a significant amount of users (mostly in developing countries) who have Intel GPUS that range from late Intel GMA (X3100+) and pre Intel HD 4000. Those GPUs are commonly found on low end devices that were made in the 2010-2015 (again, mostly on developing countries), which do not have OpenGL 3.3 suppor in the driver, but which do support the feature set because they are DX10 level hardware. The only way for these users to run Godot or Godot exports is via ANGLE.

As I think it would be very complex to ship separate versions of the editor and templates with a DLL (or the DLL separately), the ideal solution would be for us to ship the Godot Windows editor and Windows templates with ANGLE statically linked, but used only as a fallback if OpenGL 3.3 can not be found. How difficult would this be?

